    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`BUILD SUCCESS` \u2014 all 255 tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved date-derived values to a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting 1 July, labelled by the calendar year in which it ends, e.g. 2 Jul 2025 \u2192 FY26).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 the derivation logic:\n   - Added a `fiscalYearOf(LocalDate)` helper: `month \u2265 July ? year+1 : year`.\n   - Added a `fiscalYear(owner)` mapper method returning `FY<YY>` (last two digits of the fiscal year) from the business-day-adjusted `registrationDate`, plus the `@Mapping(target = \"fiscalYear\", ...)` wiring.\n   - **Membership identifier**: `membershipNumber` now uses the fiscal year for its `-M<YY>` segment instead of `registrationDate.getYear() % 100`.\n   - **Tenure**: the +3 membership-points rule now triggers on at least one *elapsed fiscal year* (`fiscalYearOf(today) - fiscalYearOf(registrationDate) >= 1`) rather than a 365-day window.\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` property (`FY<YY>`) to the Owner schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it, and updated the `membershipNumber` / `membershipPoints` descriptions to reflect the fiscal-year basis.\n\nThe new `AcceptanceTest.coreReturnsFiscalYear` (asserting `fiscalYear` matches `FY\\d{2}`) passes, and no existing tests regressed.\n\nNote: the fiscal-year label uses the standard Australian convention (labelled by the ending year). The spec/tests only pin the `FY<YY>` format, and no test pins an exact fiscal-year value, so this convention is consistent with everything that's verifiable.",
